.TH DHCPT 1 "October 2026" "dhcpt" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dhcpt \- comprehensive DHCP tester, troubleshooting, and diagnostic CLI utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhcpt
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIINTERFACE\fR]
.br
.B dhcpt
\fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B dhcpt
\fB\-\-list\-interfaces\fR
.br
.B dhcpt
\fB\-\-list\-options\fR
.br
.B dhcpt
\fB\-\-install\-skill\fR [\fITARGET\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBdhcpt\fR is a Layer 2 and Layer 3 DHCP testing, diagnostic, and troubleshooting tool
designed for Linux network engineers and system administrators. It crafts RFC-compliant
DHCP Discover packets, listens for DHCP Offers, and thoroughly decodes network parameters,
lease lifetimes, Option 82 Relay Agent parameters, and RFC 3442 Classless Static Routes.
.PP
\fBdhcpt\fR is strictly \fBlease-safe\fR: it only completes the initial Discover \(-> Offer
handshake. It never issues a DHCP Request or commits leases in DHCP server databases.
.PP
On Layer 2 Ethernet interfaces, \fBdhcpt\fR uses raw packet sockets (\fBAF_PACKET\fR)
requiring superuser privileges (\fBsudo\fR). On Layer 3 point-to-point and tunnel interfaces
without hardware MAC addresses (e.g., WireGuard \fBwg0\fR, OpenVPN \fBtun0\fR),
\fBdhcpt\fR automatically switches to raw IP sockets (\fBAF_INET\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Interface Targeting"
.TP
\fIinterface\fR, \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR, \fB\-\-interface\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR
Target network interface to send DHCP Discovers on (e.g. \fBeth0\fR, \fBens3\fR, \fBbond0\fR, \fBwg0\fR).
May be passed as an option flag or as a single positional argument.
.SS "DHCP Relay & IP-Helper Simulation (Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Linux)"
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fISERVERS\fR, \fB\-\-server\fR \fISERVERS\fR, \fB\-\-servers\fR \fISERVERS\fR
Target one or more dedicated DHCP servers by IP address or FQDN (comma-separated, e.g. \fB\-s 10.1.1.1,10.1.1.2\fR).
Simulates unicast relay forwarding (Cisco \fIip helper-address\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-subnet\fR \fIRELAY_SUBNET\fR
Specify the gateway IP of the target subnet/pool to request allocation from via RFC 3527
Link Selection (Option 82 Sub-option 5).
.TP
\fB\-\-circuit\-id\fR \fICIRCUIT_ID\fR
Simulate Option 82 Sub-option 1 Circuit ID (e.g. \fBVlan100\fR, \fBge-0/0/1\fR, RFC 3046).
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\-id\fR \fIREMOTE_ID\fR
Simulate Option 82 Sub-option 2 Remote ID (e.g. switch hostname, MAC, or DUID, RFC 3046).
.TP
\fB\-\-giaddr\fR \fIGIADDR\fR
Explicitly override the BOOTP Relay Agent Gateway IP address (\fIgiaddr\fR).
Defaults to the local interface IP address when relay simulation is enabled.
.SS "Protocol & DHCP Packet Options"
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fIOPTIONS\fR, \fB\-\-request\-options\fR \fIOPTIONS\fR
Comma-separated list of additional DHCP option codes (e.g. \fB12,26,66,67\fR) or symbolic names
(e.g. \fBhostname,tftp_server_name\fR) to append to the Parameter Request List (Option 55).
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\-default\-options\fR
Clear the default requested RFC options list. Requests only the options explicitly supplied
with \fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-request\-options\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-broadcast\fR
Do not set the BOOTP broadcast flag, requesting the DHCP server to send unicast Offers.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR \fIMAC\fR, \fB\-\-mac\fR \fIMAC\fR
Override the client hardware MAC address (format: \fBaa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff\fR) to verify static
DHCP reservations or MAC filtering rules without modifying the physical NIC.
.SS "Timing, Detection & Output Format"
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fITIMEOUT\fR
Timeout in seconds to wait for DHCP Offers (default: \fB5.0\fR).
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Listen for the full timeout duration to capture all answering DHCP servers on the segment.
Used to identify rogue or duplicate DHCP servers.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
Output results as formatted JSON for automated monitoring and scripting.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR
Enable verbose debug logging (frame assembly, socket operations, option decoding).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable informational logging.
.SS "Utilities & AI Integration"
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\-interfaces\fR
List available local network interfaces, operational states (\fBoperstate\fR, \fBcarrier\fR),
IP addresses, and MAC addresses, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\-options\fR
Display all supported RFC DHCP options and their default request status, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-skill\fR [\fITARGET\fR]
Install the bundled AI agent skill for Gemini CLI, Claude Code, or Mistral Vibe.
Supported targets are \fBgemini\fR, \fBclaude\fR, \fBmistral\fR, or \fBall\fR (default).
When \fBall\fR is selected, \fBdhcpt\fR automatically detects which AI assistants are present on the system (by inspecting configuration directories \fB~/.gemini\fR, \fB~/.claude\fR, \fB~/.vibe\fR or CLI binaries in \fBPATH\fR) and only deploys to active environments.
If a skill file or symlink already exists at the destination, \fBdhcpt\fR refuses to overwrite it unless \fB\-\-force\fR is explicitly specified.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Force overwrite of existing skill files or symlinks during \fB\-\-install\-skill\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-completion\fR {\fIzsh\fR,\fIbash\fR}
Generate shell completion script for Zsh or Bash to standard output, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print version number and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show command-line usage summary and exit.
.SH "DHCP RELAY & LAYER 3 SIMULATION"
When diagnosing DHCP issues across switches and routers, standard broadcast testing fails
because broadcasts do not cross Layer 3 boundaries.
.PP
\fBdhcpt\fR simulates a network relay agent (such as Cisco \fIip helper-address\fR) by:
.IP 1. 3
Sending a unicast UDP packet (sport 67, dport 67) to the dedicated DHCP server.
.IP 2. 3
Setting \fIgiaddr\fR to the tester's machine IP so the DHCP server routes the reply back.
.IP 3. 3
Injecting RFC 3527 Link Selection (Option 82 Sub-option 5) with the target gateway IP
(\fB\-\-relay\-subnet\fR).
.PP
This allows network administrators to test remote subnets and VLANs from anywhere with Layer 3
connectivity (including over corporate VPNs and WireGuard tunnels) without physical access
to the target broadcast domain.
.SH "EXIT CODES"
.TP
.B 0
Success: all queried DHCP servers replied, or at least one Offer was captured on broadcast.
.TP
.B 1
Failure / Timeout: zero DHCP Offers received, or permission denied.
.TP
.B 2
CLI usage / syntax error: missing interface, invalid IP/MAC, or unknown option.
.TP
.B 3
Partial response: multi-server query (\fB\-s\fR) where some servers answered but at least one timed out.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Standard local broadcast check:
.RS
.B sudo dhcpt -i eth0
.RE
.PP
Detect rogue DHCP servers on local network:
.RS
.B sudo dhcpt -i eth0 --all --timeout 5
.RE
.PP
Simulate Cisco IP-Helper against central server with Option 82:
.RS
.B sudo dhcpt -i eth0 -s 192.0.2.1 --relay-subnet 10.50.1.1 --circuit-id Vlan100
.RE
.PP
Dynamic egress interface selection across VPN:
.RS
.B IFACE=$(ip route get 10.1.1.1 | grep -oP 'dev \eK\eS+')
.br
.B sudo dhcpt -i "$IFACE" -s 10.1.1.1 --relay-subnet 10.50.1.1
.RE
.PP
Test Layer 3 WireGuard point-to-point interface:
.RS
.B sudo dhcpt -i wg0 -s 10.1.1.1 --relay-subnet 10.50.1.1
.RE
.PP
Verify static reservation by spoofing client MAC:
.RS
.B sudo dhcpt -i eth0 -m 00:11:22:33:44:55
.RE
.PP
Install AI assistant skill for Gemini CLI, Claude Code, and Mistral Vibe:
.RS
.B dhcpt --install-skill
.RE
